From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 03:14:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] jsonrpc: reply counts and reply-thread queries for
 reply threading

Add get_reply_counts() and get_reply_thread() to src/message.rs plus thin
getMessageReplyCounts/getMessageReplyThread JSON-RPC wrappers, for a
Telegram-style thread view in the web UI.

A reply is only counted when it is deliberate - it carries a quote and is
not forwarded - because mime_in_reply_to alone is set on every outgoing
message for email threading (prepare_msg_raw). The param check only
accepts a `q=`/`a=` line that starts the string or follows a single
newline after a non-newline character: a newline inside a param value is
stored doubled, so quoted text with a line starting `a=` or `q=` must not
pass for the message's own Forwarded/Quote param (it used to, which made
such a reply drop out of its own thread). Both stored forms of the reply
link are matched: the bare mid (outgoing) and the bracketed raw
In-Reply-To header (incoming). Threads are confined to one chat, so
"reply privately" answers stay out.

One SQL link condition (reply_link_sql) serves all three queries, so they
can't disagree:
- get_reply_counts() takes a batch of message IDs and returns, per
  message, the number of all replies below it (nested ones included). One
  recursive query walks down from all of them at once, collecting each
  reply link once, and the per-message counts are summed in memory: cost
  is the union of the subtrees, not messages x subtree (which took over a
  second for a page near the top of a long chain).
- get_reply_thread() walks up to the root with a recursive query (no hop
  cap; UNION ends cycles, where the root is the oldest message reached),
  then down to every reply, and returns (message, parent) pairs sorted by
  (timestamp, id) so the UI can show a list or lay out a tree. A message
  with several parents (colliding Message-IDs) gets the oldest, so it
  can't hang below its own descendant. It returns an empty list for a
  missing or deleted message.

Every join goes from the known message to its neighbour through one of
the Message-ID indexes: CROSS JOIN pins SQLite's join order (left alone
it turns joins around or scans all of msgs, depending on table
statistics) and the unary `+` on the chat_id/hidden terms keeps it off
the chat_id index. A 3000-message thread loads in ~15ms on a 300k-row
table either way.

The index on msgs.mime_in_reply_to is created with IF NOT EXISTS after
the numbered migrations instead of as migration 155: a fork must not
claim a version number upstream will use next, or databases already at
that number silently skip upstream's real migration. The slothfulchat_
prefix keeps the index name clear of upstream's.
